WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.560
What does virtualization actually change?

2
00:00:03.180 --> 00:00:04.860
It places an abstraction between

3
00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:06.260
the resource a program sees

4
00:00:06.260 --> 00:00:07.940
and the resource that implements

5
00:00:07.940 --> 00:00:09.940
it. Indirection lets the system

6
00:00:09.940 --> 00:00:12.360
control names, allocation and isolation

7
00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:14.720
while presenting a useful interface.

8
00:00:15.260 --> 00:00:17.940
Multiplexing shares one physical resource

9
00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:20.400
among several virtual entities. A

10
00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:22.760
physical processor can supply execution

11
00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:24.700
turns to several virtual processors.

12
00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:27.160
The virtual identities persist even

13
00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:28.640
when they are temporarily waiting

14
00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:29.640
for physical service.

15
00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:32.320
Imagine eight virtual machines with

16
00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:34.860
two virtual processors each, hosted

17
00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:37.000
on four physical cores. There

18
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:39.260
are sixteen virtual processors but

19
00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:40.520
at most four can execute

20
00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:42.580
simultaneously in this simplified core

21
00:00:42.580 --> 00:00:45.340
model. Scheduling chooses which four

22
00:00:45.340 --> 00:00:46.580
receive service at a given

23
00:00:46.580 --> 00:00:46.920
time.

24
00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:50.460
Overcommitment relies on virtual demand

25
00:00:50.780 --> 00:00:52.700
not always reaching its maximum

26
00:00:52.700 --> 00:00:54.600
at the same instant. If

27
00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:56.740
all sixteen virtual processors are

28
00:00:56.740 --> 00:00:58.440
busy, they contend for the

29
00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:01.220
four physical execution slots. More

30
00:01:01.220 --> 00:01:03.060
virtual names do not multiply

31
00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:04.920
the host available processor time.

32
00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:07.500
Aggregation goes the other direction:

33
00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:10.560
several underlying resources appear as

34
00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:13.100
one larger resource. Combining disk

35
00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:14.980
capacity into a logical volume

36
00:01:14.980 --> 00:01:17.240
is an example. The abstraction

37
00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:18.880
can simplify naming while the

38
00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:21.100
layout still determines usable capacity

39
00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:22.280
and failure behavior.

40
00:01:22.780 --> 00:01:24.960
For an added illustration with

41
00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:27.580
equal disks, striping uses all

42
00:01:27.580 --> 00:01:29.080
n disks of size S

43
00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:31.260
for different data, giving n

44
00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:33.920
times S capacity. Two-disk mirroring

45
00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:36.300
stores each item twice, giving

46
00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:38.640
S usable capacity. If one

47
00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:41.460
disk-equivalent stores parity information, the

48
00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:43.200
remaining data capacity is n

49
00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:45.360
minus one, multiplied by S.

50
00:01:45.620 --> 00:01:48.380
These are layout-derived capacity formulas

51
00:01:48.380 --> 00:01:50.480
under the stated equal-size assumptions.

52
00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:51.840
They are not a full

53
00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:53.820
theory of recovery, performance or

54
00:01:53.820 --> 00:01:56.400
failure independence. Match the capacity

55
00:01:56.400 --> 00:01:57.720
to what the layout stores

56
00:01:57.920 --> 00:01:59.840
rather than memorizing every combination

57
00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:00.800
as simple addition.

58
00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:03.460
Emulation presents a resource whose

59
00:02:03.460 --> 00:02:05.400
interface or behavior differs from

60
00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:07.840
the underlying implementation. A RAM

61
00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:10.360
disk exposes storage operations implemented

62
00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:13.160
in memory, while cross-architecture translation

63
00:02:13.160 --> 00:02:16.280
presents another instruction environment. One

64
00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:19.200
system can combine emulation, aggregation

65
00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:20.360
and multiplexing.

66
00:02:20.740 --> 00:02:23.240
For each exam example, identify

67
00:02:23.240 --> 00:02:25.420
the exported abstraction and the

68
00:02:25.420 --> 00:02:28.240
implementing resources first. Then explain

69
00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:30.000
whether the key operation shares

70
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.760
one resource, combines several, or

71
00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:35.120
presents a different interface. A

72
00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:36.960
familiar product name alone is

73
00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:37.860
not the mechanism.

74
00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:40.000
Things to take note of.

75
00:02:40.520 --> 00:02:44.340
Multiplexing shares, aggregation combines, and

76
00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:46.320
emulation supplies a different apparent

77
00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:49.240
resource. Count physical capacity separately

78
00:02:49.240 --> 00:02:51.320
from virtual identities, and state

79
00:02:51.320 --> 00:02:53.540
assumptions before deriving any capacity

80
00:02:53.540 --> 00:02:53.960
formula.
